  "contents": "Is this clause enforceable in a residential lease in Ontario? Cite the section.\n\nRead the clause as a term of an ordinary private residential tenancy in Ontario that the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 covers: the rental unit has no exemption under section 6.1, and it is not student housing, a care home, a co-operative or a mobile home park.\n\nClause: On signing, the Tenant shall give the Landlord a signed blank cheque, to be kept as security and returned uncashed when the tenancy ends.\n\nEnd your answer with these two lines:\nVerdict: enforceable (or: Verdict: not enforceable)\nSection: <the number of the one section of the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 that decides it>\n",
  "config": {
   "max_output_tokens": 16000
  }
 },
 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T20:15:30.826428Z",
 "text": "Under the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* (RTA), this clause is void and unenforceable. \n\nSection 2(1) of the RTA defines a \"security deposit\" broadly to include not just money, but also any \"property or a right paid or given by, or on behalf of, a tenant ... to a landlord ... to be held by or for the landlord as security for the performance of an obligation or the payment of a liability of the tenant or to be returned to the tenant upon the happening of a condition.\" A signed blank cheque given to be held as security and returned at the end of the tenancy falls squarely within this definition.\n\nUnder **section 105**, the *only* security deposit a landlord is permitted to collect is a lawful rent deposit (for the last rent period) collected in accordance with section 106. Because requiring a signed blank cheque as security violates section 105, the lease term is inconsistent with the Act and is void (per section 4(1)).\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 105",
 "finish_reason": "STOP",
 "input_tokens": 190,
 "output_tokens": 1903,
